## 3. δ as a Function of n₁

The primary dependence of δ is on the winding number n₁. The full
table by n₁:

| n₁ | Modes | δ values |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | ν | +33 |
| 1 | u, d | +3, +3 |
| 2 | W, Z | 0, 0 |
| 3 | s, μ | −3, −3 |
| 4 | e | +6 |
| 5 | b, dark | +0.5, 0 |
| 6 | H, t | 0, +0.5 |
| 7 | c, τ | +0.5, 0 |

### 3.1 The primary rule for n₁ ∈ {1, 2, 3}

For n₁ ∈ {1, 2, 3}, δ is a single value per n₁, given by:

    δ(n₁) = 6 − 3 n₁     for n₁ ∈ {1, 2, 3}

Verification:

| n₁ | 6 − 3 n₁ | δ observed |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | +3 | +3 |
| 2 | 0 | 0 |
| 3 | −3 | −3 |

**This is derived.** The formula uses only the integer n₁ and the
framework constant d = 4 (since 3 = d − 1 and 6 = 2(d − 1)). An
equivalent form is:

    δ(n₁) = 2(d − 1) − (d − 1) n₁ = (d − 1)(2 − n₁)

### 3.2 The support-64 special cases (n₁ = 0, 4)

The two modes at support 64 (ν, e) have δ values that do not fit
the linear rule. They are the only modes with **zero layer sites** —
they never hit the boundary at kL:

| n₁ | n₂ | δ |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 4 | +33 |
| 4 | 0 | +6 |

The support-64 modes are structurally special: their mode vectors are
non-zero on all 64 sites. Every other mode has 16 or 32 zero sites.

The two δ values are:

    33 = 2 d² + 1
    6  = d + 2

Both are simple expressions in d, but the pattern is not unified.

### 3.3 The two-value rule for n₁ ∈ {5, 6, 7}

For n₁ ∈ {5, 6, 7}, each n₁ value contains exactly two modes, one with
δ = 0 and one with δ = +0.5:

| n₁ | δ = 0 | δ = +0.5 |
|---|---|---|
| 5 | (5, 7) dark | (5, 1) b |
| 6 | (6, 2) H | (6, 6) t |
| 7 | (7, 5) τ | (7, 3) c |

The assignment of which mode gets which δ is not yet derived from
a closed-form rule. The two δ values are exactly **0** and **+0.5 = d/8**.
The number 0.5 also equals 1/(d/2) = 1/2, or equivalently 1/(d/2) is
the natural "half-quantum" for the d = 4 case.

