## 5. Structural Interpretation

The δ values are the deviations from the base formulas `b(H) + a(d)`.
The base formulas are determined by the framework constants. The δ
values are corrections that account for the specific structure of
each mode on the torus.

The dominant dependence is on the **winding number n₁**, which the
framework already identifies as the "processing direction" of the mesh
(the shell rule uses n₁ as well). The linear rule δ = (d − 1)(2 − n₁)
for n₁ ∈ {1, 2, 3} is the direct expression of this: as the mode winds
more in the n₁ direction, δ decreases by d − 1 = 3 per unit.

The support-64 modes (n₁ = 0, 4) are the exception because they never
hit the layer. Their δ values are set by a different mechanism — the
mode is entirely delocalized, and the correction reflects the absence
of layer interaction.

The n₁ ∈ {5, 6, 7} modes have δ ∈ {0, +0.5}, suggesting they are in
a different regime where the linear rule no longer applies. The value
0.5 = d/8 is small, suggesting these modes are close to their base
values.

